/* ==========================================================================
   ACCESSIBILITY

   Three preferences that change how the whole interface behaves rather than
   how one control looks, so they are written as classes on the root of a
   document instead of on any element in it.

   Two of them are here and not in code because they have to be: font sizes
   and transition durations are tokens, a token is a custom property, and the
   only way to give a custom property a different value for part of the tree
   is a rule. There is no C# for it.

   The third - the scale of the interface as a whole - is not here, because it
   is not a style. It is the panel's reference resolution, and it lives in
   UiPreferences.cs beside the other two so that all three are applied in one
   place and nobody has to find out the hard way that two of them are.
   ========================================================================== */

/* ==========================================================================
   TEXT SIZE

   The type ladder, four times over. Independent of the interface scale on
   purpose: those are two different complaints. "The buttons are too small to
   hit" is answered by scaling everything; "I cannot read that" is answered
   here, and answering it by scaling everything spends screen the game needs.

   The steps are the ladder multiplied and rounded, not a free scale factor.
   A font size that lands on a fraction of a pixel is a font size that renders
   soft, and the whole ladder has to stay in proportion to itself - which is
   what a table of values guarantees and a multiplier does not.

   Normal has no class. It is the ladder as Tokens.uss declares it, and a rule
   that restates every value it inherits is a second copy to keep in step.
   ========================================================================== */

.text--small {
    --font-size-xs: 12px;
    --font-size-s: 14px;
    --font-size-m: 17px;
    --font-size-l: 29px;
    --font-size-xl: 48px;
}


.text--large {
    --font-size-xs: 15px;
    --font-size-s: 19px;
    --font-size-m: 23px;
    --font-size-l: 39px;
    --font-size-xl: 64px;
}


.text--largest {
    --font-size-xs: 17px;
    --font-size-s: 22px;
    --font-size-m: 27px;
    --font-size-l: 44px;
    --font-size-xl: 72px;
}


/* ==========================================================================
   REDUCED MOTION

   Everything in this interface arrives rather than appears: a screen fades, a
   column slides in from its edge, a panel rises while it grows. That reads as
   a place being opened, and for a player who gets motion sick or who simply
   cannot follow a moving target it reads as the interface being unusable.

   Nothing is removed - every screen still opens, closes and answers the
   pointer, and every end state is the state it already had. What goes is the
   time in between, which is the part that moves.

   Said as durations rather than by turning transitions off. A transition
   with no properties still has to be written per rule; a duration of zero is
   one line that catches every rule in the theme, including the ones written
   after this file.
   ========================================================================== */

.motion--reduced {
    --motion-fast: 0s;
    --motion-normal: 0s;
    --motion-screen: 0s;
}


/* The entrance offsets, which are not durations and so survive a zeroed
   transition: a panel that starts at 0.97 and never animates would simply sit
   there slightly small and slightly low. Everything begins where it ends. */

.motion--reduced .panel,
.motion--reduced .overlay .panel {
    scale: 1 1;
    translate: 0 0;
}


.motion--reduced .enter {
    translate: 0 0;

    opacity: 1;
}


/* Staggered arrival is nothing but delay, and delay is the thing being asked
   for less of. */

.motion--reduced .enter--2,
.motion--reduced .enter--3,
.motion--reduced .enter--4 {
    transition-delay: 0s;
}
